<section class="front-hero page-shell" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  <div class="hero-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">Independent engineering &amp; research</p>
    <h1 id="hero-title">Systems first. <em>Models second.</em></h1>
    <p class="hero-lede">A model can produce an answer. Getting useful work done takes more. We build the software around the model: the tools it can use, the context it needs, the machines that run the work, and the records that show what happened. We also build standalone tools for software engineering, communication, planning, and technical learning. The real test comes after the demo—when a provider changes, a connection drops, or a person needs to take control.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="button button-primary" href="{{ site.basePath }}/work/">Explore our work <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a class="quiet-link" href="{{ site.basePath }}/projects/">Browse software</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="conditions-feature" aria-labelledby="conditions-title">
    <div class="conditions-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Engineering under real conditions</p>
      <h2 id="conditions-title">Constraints are part of the design.</h2>
      <p>Good software has to work under the conditions people actually have. These six questions recur across our engineering.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="condition-sheet">
      <p class="condition-sheet-label">Questions we ask before choosing a design</p>
      <ol class="condition-list">
        <li><span class="condition-index">01</span><span class="condition-term">Compute</span><span class="condition-question">What can run on the hardware available?</span></li>
        <li><span class="condition-index">02</span><span class="condition-term">Providers</span><span class="condition-question">What can we replace without rebuilding?</span></li>
        <li><span class="condition-index">03</span><span class="condition-term">Connectivity</span><span class="condition-question">What works when a connection fails?</span></li>
        <li><span class="condition-index">04</span><span class="condition-term">Platforms</span><span class="condition-question">What changes between machines?</span></li>
        <li><span class="condition-index">05</span><span class="condition-term">Authority</span><span class="condition-question">Who can act, approve, or stop the work?</span></li>
        <li><span class="condition-index">06</span><span class="condition-term">Evidence</span><span class="condition-question">What can someone else verify?</span></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="condition-sheet-note">The operating conditions shape the design.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</section>

<section class="research-section" aria-labelledby="directions-title">
  <div class="page-shell">
    <div class="research-intro">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Current engineering directions</p>
        <h2 id="directions-title">Two problems at the center of our research.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Models can help decide what to do. Real systems also have to keep context, set limits, and run authorized work. ForgeWire and Fabric tackle different parts of that problem.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="research-projects">
      <article class="research-project">
        <div class="research-project-heading"><h3><a href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview">Intelligent systems · ForgeWire <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h3><span class="status status--private">Private development</span></div>
        <p>ForgeWire brings models, tools, projects, and selected multi-step workflows into one workspace. We're working on continuity beyond any one chat or provider, with ways for people to direct and inspect the work.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="{{ site.basePath }}/engineering/">Explore ForgeWire <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </article>
      <article class="research-project">
        <div class="research-project-heading"><h3><a href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview/blob/85d1905b0bd8db420882f00065daff34833dab27/README.md#3-fabric-dispatch-and-remote-execution">Distributed execution · Fabric <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h3><span class="status status--private">Private development</span></div>
        <p>Fabric is a separate control plane for getting authorized work to remote machines. Authentication, policy, and execution boundaries belong in the infrastructure—not in a model's promise to behave. Selected ForgeWire workflows use private, configuration-dependent integration paths.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="{{ site.basePath }}/engineering/">Explore Fabric <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

